# Clean Code — Naming

## NAME-01 — Choose descriptive and unambiguous names

A name should answer why it exists, what it does, and how it is used. A name that requires a comment to explain it has already failed.

```
// bad
int d  // elapsed time in days

// good
int elapsedDays
```

## NAME-02 — Make meaningful distinctions

Do not create names that differ only in arbitrary noise. If two things are different, their names must express the difference.

```
// bad — what is the distinction between these two?
function getActiveAccount()
function getActiveAccountInfo()

// bad — number suffixes communicate nothing
copyChars(a1, a2)

// good — names express the actual roles
copyChars(source, destination)
```

## NAME-03 — Use pronounceable names

If you cannot say the name aloud in a conversation, it will slow down code reviews and discussion.

```
// bad
genymdhms   // generation year/month/day/hour/minute/second

// good
generationTimestamp
```

## NAME-04 — Use searchable names

Avoid single-letter names and bare numeric literals for anything other than short loop counters. A name must be findable with a text search.

```
// bad
for i = 0 to 5
  sum += values[i] * 4

// good
WORK_DAYS_PER_WEEK = 5
MULTIPLIER = 4

for day = 0 to WORK_DAYS_PER_WEEK
  sum += values[day] * MULTIPLIER
```

## NAME-05 — Replace magic numbers with named constants

A bare numeric literal in logic is opaque to every reader, including the original author six months later.

```
// bad
if age >= 18

// good
LEGAL_ADULT_AGE = 18
if age >= LEGAL_ADULT_AGE
```

## NAME-06 — Replace magic strings with named constants or enums

A bare string literal carries the same problems as a magic number — it is invisible to search, easy to mistype, and carries no documented meaning.

Named constants remove the risk of typos but still allow any string value at the call site. When the set of valid values is **closed and known**, prefer an **enum** (or union type) — the type system then rejects invalid values entirely.

```
// bad — bare string literals scattered across logic
if user.role == "admin"
  grantAccess()
end if

if order.status == "pending"
  sendReminder()
end if
```

```
// better — named constants eliminate typos
ROLE_ADMIN     = "admin"
STATUS_PENDING = "pending"

if user.role == ROLE_ADMIN
  grantAccess()
end if

if order.status == STATUS_PENDING
  sendReminder()
end if
```

```
// best — enum closes the set; invalid values are rejected at compile time
enum Role
  ADMIN
  EDITOR
  VIEWER
end enum

enum OrderStatus
  PENDING
  CONFIRMED
  SHIPPED
  CANCELLED
end enum

if user.role == Role.ADMIN
  grantAccess()
end if

if order.status == OrderStatus.PENDING
  sendReminder()
end if
```

Use a union/literal type when your language does not have enums but supports closed string unions:

```
// union type (e.g. TypeScript-style)
type OrderStatus = "pending" | "confirmed" | "shipped" | "cancelled"

function sendReminder(status: OrderStatus)
  if status == "pending"   // compiler rejects any other value
    notify()
  end if
end function
```

## NAME-07 — Avoid encodings and type prefixes

Do not append type information or Hungarian-notation prefixes. Types are visible in modern tooling; encoding them into names adds noise without information.

```
// bad
strUserName = "Alice"
iRetryCount = 3
class IPaymentGateway

// good
userName = "Alice"
retryCount = 3
class PaymentGateway
```

## NAME-08 — One word per concept, one concept per word

Pick a single word for each concept and use it consistently throughout the codebase. Do not use `send`, `write`, and `transmit` as synonyms for the same operation. Equally, do not overload one word to cover two distinct operations — `activate` should not mean both "set a flag" and "send a network message".

## NAME-09 — Prefix booleans with `is`, `has`, or `can`

Boolean variables and functions read like prose when prefixed correctly. Always prefer positive forms to avoid confusing double negations.

```
// bad
if not isEmpty(list)
if disabled
if wasProcessed

// good
if list.hasElements()
if isEnabled
if isSent
```

For collections, use `isEach` / `isAny` to express all-or-any semantics:

```
// bad
if allLightsOn

// good
if isEachLightOn
if isAnyLightOn
```

## NAME-10 — Follow subprogram naming conventions

- **Procedures** (may or may not return a value) should start with a verb: `syncViews()`, `list.addItem(x)`.
- **Functions** (always return a value) should describe their result: `elapsedTime()`, `sin(x)`, `isFile(path)`.
- **Class methods** must not repeat or include the class name: write `Line.length()`, not `Line.getLineLength()`.

> A single-noun function implies a pure computation. Never let `x.length()` modify state.

## NAME-11 — Scale name length to scope size

Variables with a large scope need long, descriptive names. Variables in a small, local scope may use short names because the context is immediately visible.

```
// bad — short name for a module-level variable
n = fetchUsers()

// bad — verbose name for a three-line loop counter
for iterationIndex = 0 to list.length
  process(list[iterationIndex])
end for

// good
totalActiveUsers = fetchUsers()

for i = 0 to list.length
  process(list[i])
end for
```

## NAME-12 — Apply consistent conventions for collections, counts, and constants

- **Collections** (arrays, lists, maps) take a plural name: `cars`, `indices`, `errorMessages`.
- **Count variables** use a `num` or `n` prefix: `numCars`, `nRetries`.
- **Constants** are written in `UPPER_CASE`: `MAX_RETRIES`, `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS`.

```
// bad
car_list = []
count = 0
maxRetries = 3

// good
cars = []
numCars = 0
MAX_RETRIES = 3
```

## NAME-13 — Use symmetric word pairs for opposites

When naming complementary operations or states, choose antonym pairs that are equally recognisable and, where possible, equal in length. Mixing pairs (`start`/`end` instead of `start`/`stop`) forces readers to infer the relationship.

Common pairs:

| `set` | `get` | `send` | `recv` | `open` | `close` | `push` | `pop` |
|-------|-------|--------|--------|--------|---------|--------|-------|
| `insert` | `delete` | `attach` | `detach` | `show` | `hide` | `start` | `stop` |
